  { question: "How long do I have to file an amputation injury claim in Jackson County?", answer: "Time limits vary by case type. Personal injury claims against at-fault parties generally have a three-year statute of limitations. Workers' compensation claims require reporting your injury within 90 days and filing within two years. No-fault auto accident benefits must be applied for within one year of the accident. Product liability claims may have different deadlines. Don't delay—contact an attorney immediately to preserve all your rights and claims." },
  { question: "Can I sue if I lost a limb in a workplace accident in Jackson County?", answer: "Workers' compensation is typically your exclusive remedy against your direct employer. However, you can pursue third-party liability claims against general contractors, subcontractors, equipment manufacturers, property owners, or other negligent parties whose actions contributed to your amputation. These third-party claims provide compensation beyond workers' compensation, including full pain and suffering damages. Our attorneys identify all potentially liable parties to pursue the full compensation you're owed." },
  { question: "What are Michigan's specific loss benefits for amputations?", answer: "Michigan workers' compensation law provides specific loss benefits—predetermined compensation amounts based on the body part lost—in addition to medical coverage and wage loss benefits. These benefits recognize the permanent nature of limb loss and provide financial security. The amount depends on which limb or digit was amputated and your average weekly wage. Our attorneys ensure you receive the full specific loss benefits you're entitled to under Michigan law." },
  { question: "How do you prove the full value of an amputation injury case?", answer: "We work with life care planners who calculate lifetime medical needs including prosthetic replacements, adjustments, and maintenance. Vocational experts assess your lost earning capacity over your entire working life. Economists project these costs adjusted for inflation. Medical experts testify about your permanent limitations and ongoing care requirements. We document not just current expenses but decades of future costs to ensure your settlement or verdict provides true financial security." },
